  "question": {
    "text": "In the estimation of bromine, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of an organic compound gave <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of silver bromide. The percentage of bromine in the given compound is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>%</mo></math> (nearest integer)<br />(Relative atomic masses of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ag</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Br</mi></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>108</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>80</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi></math>, respectively)."
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "34",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The estimation of halogens in organic compounds is usually done by carius method.</p><p>Percentage of Bromine&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Atomic</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mass</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Br</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Molecular</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mass</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>AgBr</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Mass</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>AgBr</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>Mass</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>the</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>organic</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>compound</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>188</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>34</mn><mo>%</mo></math></p>"
